<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">235k</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">46.0</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">38.3</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">39.5</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">40.2</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">40.5</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">41.3</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">41.5</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">88.1</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">69.4</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">71.5</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.13" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">73.3</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.14" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">74.9</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.15" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">76.9</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.18.16" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 8.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.18.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0F0F0;">77.8</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Truncated Speech-to-Text Speculation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the truncated speech <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we need the corresponding translated text to form a truncated speech-text pair. While manual annotation is straightforward, it is time-consuming, costly, and difficult to scale. Therefore, we leverage the capabilities of foundational LALMs to automatically retrieve the translated text for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since LALMs generate translated text in an autoregressive manner, the translation corresponding to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> must be in the form <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{y}_{1:t^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{1:t^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the text truncation point, with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq t^{\prime}\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq t^{\prime}\leq t</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, determining <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{y}_{1:t^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{1:t^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> based on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is essential.
From the perspective of the target text, all information in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{y}_{1:t^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{1:t^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> must be presented in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while maximizing the retention of translatable content. Thus, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must meet the following conditions: (1) For each <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{i}(1\leq i\leq t^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{i}(1\leq i\leq t^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, there must be corresponding information in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>; (2) For <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{t^{\prime}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{t^{\prime}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, there should be no corresponding information in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Excluding the content already translated in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{y}_{1:t^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{1:t^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> leaves the remaining speech information, indicating what still needs to be translated later. This forms a speech-text pair that captures the “wait” signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Building on the above analysis, we propose the Speech-to-Text Speculation technique to automatically create truncated speech-to-text pairs, drawing inspiration from Speculative Decoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
Specifically, we iteratively concatenate <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{y}_{1:i}~(i=1,2,...,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{1:i}~(i=1,2,...,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and input them along with the prompt to the LALMs to obtain the probability distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{p}(\cdot|\text{prompt},\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}},\bm{y}_{1:i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext>prompt</mtext><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}(\cdot|\text{prompt},\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}},\bm{y}_{1:i})</annotation></semantics></math> for the next token.
We believe that if the information of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has appeared in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>: (1) its corresponding probability should be higher than that of <span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;EOS&gt;</span>; (2) the probability ranking of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> should be among the top tokens in the vocabulary, indicating lower uncertainty from the LALMs.
Therefore, at each iteration, we determine the termination rule based on the following criteria:
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{p}(y_{i})&lt;\bm{p}(\texttt{&lt;EOS&gt;})~~\bigcup" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒑</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒑</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathsize="0.900em">&lt;EOS&gt;</mtext><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.787em">)</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">⋃</mo></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{p}(y_{i})&lt;\bm{p}(\texttt{&lt;EOS&gt;})~~\bigcup</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|}\sum_{\text{token}\in\mathcal{V}}\mathbb{I}\left\{\bm{p}(y_{i})&lt;\bm{p}(\text{token})\right\}&gt;\tau," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒱</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">token</mtext><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝕀</mi><mo>{</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒑</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒑</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">token</mtext><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo><mo>}</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|}\sum_{\text{token}\in\mathcal{V}}\mathbb{I}\left\{\bm{p}(y_{i})&lt;\bm{p}(\text{token})\right\}&gt;\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the position threshold. In the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration, if the probability corresponding to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the Eq.<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.2 Truncated Speech-to-Text Speculation ‣ 3 Method ‣ Direct Simultaneous Translation Activation for Large Audio-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, iteration will terminate, and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}},\bm{y}_{1:i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x}_{1:s^{\prime}},\bm{y}_{1:i})</annotation></semantics></math> will be the final constructed speech-text pair. Otherwise, we continue to the next iteration with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> until termination.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the dataset, we use the CoVoST2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, which contains speech and text pairs. Specifically, we use English as the source language and Chinese as the target language. The training set includes 364 hours of audio with 232,341 samples, while the test set has 25 hours of audio with 15,531 samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the base model, we use Qwen2-Audio-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and apply SimulSA and SFT to its base version<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2-Audio-7B" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2-Audio-7B</a></span></span></span>. For SFT, we utilize LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> with a rank of 8 and an alpha of 32. During training, we set the batch size to 128, the learning rate to 1e-4, and the weight decay to 0.1. We train the model using the <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ms-swift</span> framework<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/modelscope/ms-swift" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/modelscope/ms-swift</a></span></span></span> and use the checkpoint from the second training epoch for evaluation. We follow the template similar to Qwen2-Audio:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="88.66" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 303.15 88.66" width="303.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,88.66) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 84.03 C 0 86.58 2.07 88.66 4.63 88.66 L 298.52 88.66 C 301.08 88.66 303.15 86.58 303.15 84.03 L 303.15 4.63 C 303.15 2.07 301.08 0 298.52 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 84.03 C 0.69 86.2 2.45 87.96 4.63 87.96 L 298.52 87.96 C 300.69 87.96 302.46 86.2 302.46 84.03 L 302.46 4.63 C 302.46 2.45 300.69 0.69 298.52 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 15.96)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:18.96em;--ltx-fo-height:4.35em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="63.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 60.19)" width="262.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:18.96em;">
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{&lt;audio&gt;}\{\text{your\_speech\_file\_path}\}\texttt{&lt;/audio&gt;}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;audio&gt;</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">your_speech_file_path</mtext><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;/audio&gt;</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{&lt;audio&gt;}\{\text{your\_speech\_file\_path}\}\texttt{&lt;/audio&gt;}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Detect the language and translate the speech into Mandarin: <span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|en|&gt;</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For evaluation, we test Simul-S2TT with chunk sizes <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of 500, 1000, 1500, 2000, 3000, and 4000 ms. We also perform offline translation by setting <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to infinity, translating after all speech is received for the best performance. In practice, we use a rollback strategy by removing the last <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> tokens after each step, testing <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> (no rollback). We measure BLEU scores <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> using <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SacreBLEU<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">3</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/mjpost/sacrebleu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url">https://github.com/mjpost/sacrebleu</a></span></span></span></span> and xCOMET scores <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> using <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">XCOMET-XXL<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">4</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/Unbabel/XCOMET-XXL" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url">https://huggingface.co/Unbabel/XCOMET-XXL</a></span></span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For hyper-parameters, the sampling interval <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[l,r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l,r]</annotation></semantics></math> in speech truncation is <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=500ms" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>500</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=500ms</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=5000ms" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5000</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=5000ms</annotation></semantics></math>, or the maximum feature count if needed.
The position threshold <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=100/v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=100/v</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=151{,}646" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>151,646</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=151{,}646</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=6.6\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6.6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=6.6\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The augmented data size <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 3,000. An ablation study on this is in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Self-Augmentation Data Size ‣ 5 Ablation and Analysis ‣ Direct Simultaneous Translation Activation for Large Audio-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Speech Truncation ‣ 3 Method ‣ Direct Simultaneous Translation Activation for Large Audio-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the main results of each model under varying inference settings. Our analysis yields the following findings:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low-Cost for High-Improvement.</span>
Augmenting SFT with SimulSA increases the corpus only slightly (232k → 235k) yet yields substantial BLEU gains. For a fixed chunk size, improvements are largest at smaller rollback sizes, which use fewer inference tokens. For example, with chunk size 1500, BLEU improves over SFT by +7.4 (rollback 0), +1.7 (rollback 3), and +0.8 (rollback 5). This trend holds across chunk sizes, showing that <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a small amount of SimulSA data plus our training strategy markedly improves simultaneous translation with minimal additional cost.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low-Latency Benefit Most.</span>
As chunk size decreases (lower latency), gains grow. At rollback 0, SimulSA+SFT improves BLEU by +7.2, +8.6, and +7.4 for chunk sizes 500, 1000, and 1500, versus +5.0 and +2.3 for chunk sizes 3000 and 4000. This underscores the effectiveness of our speech-truncation strategy (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Speech Truncation ‣ 3 Method ‣ Direct Simultaneous Translation Activation for Large Audio-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) in low-latency regimes: <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gains are modest at large chunks (high latency) but substantial at small chunks.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robust Offline MT.</span>
Although simultaneous and offline MT distributions differ (Table <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Direct Simultaneous Translation Activation for Large Audio-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), adding SimulSA does not harm offline MT: at <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, BLEU changes within random variation. Hence, <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SimulSA improves simultaneous MT without compromising offline MT, ensuring robustness and reliability for real-world applications.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Ablation and Analysis</h2>

<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15692v1/augsize.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:405/383;" width="405" height="383"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Ablation of self-augmentation data size for different <math id="S5.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Self-Augmentation Data Size</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To thoroughly investigate how the size of SimulSA data (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>) affects performance, we conduct an ablation study using <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{1000,2000,3000\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{1000,2000,3000\}</annotation></semantics></math>, performing SFT for each case. BLEU scores were measured for each <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> under the settings of</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{k|k=500,1000,1500,2000\}\times\{b|b=0,3,5\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1500</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2000</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{k|k=500,1000,1500,2000\}\times\{b|b=0,3,5\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Ablation and Analysis ‣ Direct Simultaneous Translation Activation for Large Audio-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, increasing the amount of augmented data consistently boosts BLEU scores across all <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> values. When <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=0</annotation></semantics></math> (no rollback), BLEU improvements scale almost linearly with additional data, suggesting further gains as more augmented data is added. For <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> (higher <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> means greater inference latency), BLEU improvements plateau once the augmented data reaches about 1.3% of the original dataset. This saturation indicates that only a modest increase in augmented data is needed to meaningfully enhance the performance of these stronger baselines.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15692v1/distribution_variants.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/270;" width="476" height="270"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Variants of speech truncation distribution: <span id="S5.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Original</span>—continuous beta decay within <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[l,r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l,r]</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="S5.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variant 1</span>—continuous uniform within <math id="S5.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[l,r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l,r]</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="S5.F4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variant 2</span>—continuous beta decay over the full speech span; <span id="S5.F4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variant 3</span>—discrete beta decay every 500ms within <math id="S5.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[l,r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l,r]</annotation></semantics></math>. The shaded area indicates the truncation sampling interval.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>BLEU scores for Original and Variant 1–3 truncation distributions across speech chunk sizes (<math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=500,1000,1500,2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1500</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=500,1000,1500,2000</annotation></semantics></math>) and rollback values (0, 3, 5).
</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:64.3pt;vertical-align:-30.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-25.0pt,4.7pt) scale(0.873441913763202,0.873441913763202) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding:1pt 2.8pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
